 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "LOCH NIS · LOCH NESS. 37 km long, about 230 m deep, and holding more fresh water than all the lakes of England and Wales together. The peat stains it the colour of strong tea. Whatever's in there has an excellent hiding place."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "URQUHART CASTLE (Caisteal Urchadain). A fortress on this headland for 700 years. In 1692 the garrison blew up their own gatehouse so the Jacobites couldn't use it. Very thorough. Not very tidy."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "THE MONSTER EXHIBITION. Admission includes a talking sea serpent, a sonar room and a gift shop with 41 different Nessies. The one in the window is smiling. It may know something."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "NESS SURVEY JETTY. Authorised vessels only. Keep a hand on your hat, a hand on your phone, and a hand on your sandwiches. That's three hands. Seagulls have more."
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "DEEP WATER. The loch drops off sharply here. The sign is a request. The water is not a path."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 16,
   "plaque": "loch_ness_1933_sighting",
   "title": "The 1933 Sighting",
   "text": "The modern monster began in 1933, just as a new road was finished along the north shore. That May the Inverness Courier reported a local couple seeing an enormous animal rolling in the water, and called it a monster. More cars meant more eyes, and the rest is merchandise."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 16,
   "plaque": "loch_ness_surgeons_photograph",
   "title": "The Surgeon's Photograph",
   "text": "The most famous Nessie picture, a long neck rising from the water, appeared in the Daily Mail in 1934, credited to a London surgeon. In 1994 it was revealed as a hoax: the monster was a toy submarine with a sculpted head. It fooled the world for sixty years. Nessie was not consulted."
  }
 ],
